20261110:
	/etc/profile no longer exports LD_LIBRARY_PATH (NetBSD does not
	either).  The dynamic linker searches LD_LIBRARY_PATH before a
	library's RPATH, so the old setting (/usr/lib ahead of /usr/pkg/lib)
	made base libraries shadow same-named packaged ones in login shells
	- e.g. the base libgcc_s.so.1 stub replaced the real one in
	/usr/pkg/lib.  Base libraries live in /usr/lib, which is always
	searched.  Software installed elsewhere without an RPATH (built
	with -Wl,-R<libdir>) now needs LD_LIBRARY_PATH set by the user.
	/etc/profile is not replaced on update: remove the export line by
	hand to pick this up.

	The bochs display driver (/dev/drm0) implements DRM-lite interface
	version 2: buffer sharing (PRIME, see <sys/ioc_mdrm.h>).  Buffers
	are reference counted and named by per-client handles;
	MDRM_DESTROY_DUMB now only drops the caller's handle;
	MDRM_DUP_HANDLE gives a buffer a second handle.  Up to 64 opens
	(dma-buf fds count).

	<sys/fcntl.h> declares F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, <string.h> strdup() and
	strndup(), and <sys/stat.h> fchmod() for POSIX.1-2008 compilations
	(_POSIX_C_SOURCE >= 200809L; F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC also _XOPEN_SOURCE >=
	700), not only with _NETBSD_SOURCE/_XOPEN_SOURCE.  Rebuild and install the driver with
	make hdboot.

	<pthread.h> (libmthread): pthread_t values are now the mthread
	thread number plus 2, so 0 never names a thread (the main thread is
	1); libstdc++'s std::thread treats 0 as "no thread".  Programs that
	mix pthread_* and mthread_* thread IDs must not compare them.  New:
	pthread_rwlock_tryrdlock()/trywrlock(), and sched_yield() works for
	programs using <sched.h> (libc has a no-op __libc_thr_yield(), which
	libmthread overrides with a thread switch).  The pthread prototypes
	now have POSIX const qualifiers (pthread_setspecific,
	pthread_*_init, pthread_create, pthread_attr_get*).  Rebuild
	programs linked statically against libmthread.

	__cxa_finalize() in libc now also runs and removes the plain
	atexit() handlers that belong to a shared object being unloaded,
	as FreeBSD does.  A dlopen()ed object that called atexit() and was
	then dlclose()d (Mesa's DRI drivers, via eglTerminate()) used to
	leave a handler pointing into unmapped memory, and the program
	crashed in exit().  Dynamically linked programs pick this up;
	static ones must be relinked.

20261108:
	<minix/mthread.h>, and so <pthread.h>, no longer includes
	<minix/config.h> and <minix/const.h>; programs that used their
	macros (EXTERN, TRUE, FALSE, ...) through <pthread.h> must include
	them themselves.  libmthread gained pthread_cond_timedwait() and
	pthread_condattr_setclock()/getclock() (CLOCK_REALTIME and
	CLOCK_MONOTONIC); the condition variable structure changed, so
	recompile programs linked statically against libmthread.

	calloc() in libc no longer clears memory that is fresh from the
	kernel (pages above the highest break ever reached); large, mostly
	unused calloc()s no longer force all their pages into memory.
	Dynamically linked programs pick this up; static ones must be
	relinked.

20261107:
	The input server now also offers Linux evdev compatible devices:
	/dev/input/event0 (all keyboards) and /dev/input/event1 (all mice),
	see <minix/evdev.h>.  While one is open, it receives all input of
	its kind and the console does not.  The PS/2 driver (pckbd) now
	reports mouse Y movement downward-positive, as the input server's
	HID conventions say (it used to pass the PS/2 upward-positive value
	through).  Rebuild and install input and pckbd together (make
	hdboot), and create the new nodes on an existing system with:

		# cd /dev && MAKEDEV input

	poll(2) in libc now treats an epoll(7) descriptor in its set as
	readable when one of the epoll instance's members is, so epoll
	descriptors can be polled and added to other epoll instances
	(select(2) on them still never fires).  Epoll instances are now
	identified by their descriptor's file, so dup(2)ed epoll descriptors
	work too.  Dynamically linked programs pick this up; statically
	linked ones must be relinked.

	Also new in libc: posix_fadvise(2) (accepts and ignores the advice)
	and strchrnul(3); <assert.h> defines C11's static_assert.

	<pthread.h>/libmthread: pthread_mutexattr_settype() now works, with
	PTHREAD_MUTEX_NORMAL, _RECURSIVE (now 1, was 0) and _ERRORCHECK;
	mutexes and condition variables accept attribute arguments, and
	zero-filled ones are initialized on first use.  Programs built
	against the old <minix/mthread.h> macros must be recompiled to get
	recursive mutexes.

	The bochs display driver now stamps page-flip events with
	CLOCK_MONOTONIC as clock_gettime(2) defines it (boot time plus
	uptime); install it with the rest of the boot image.

	Kernel and VM fix for a long-standing bug: the kernel's temporary
	mappings for copies between address spaces could be left pointing at
	page tables that VM had freed, which could make later copies into a
	process (system call results) silently go to the wrong memory.  The
	kernel and VM must be updated together: rebuild and install the boot
	image (make hdboot) and reboot.

	Kernel fix for random whole-system freezes (seen under KVM): the
	idle loop could halt the CPU with interrupts disabled.  Rebuild and
	install the boot image (make hdboot) and reboot.

20261031:
	VM now supports writable MAP_SHARED mappings of files (refused with
	EACCES unless the fd is open for writing).  This changed the VM <-> VFS
	protocol (FDLOOKUP reply flags, new VMVFSREQ_FDIO_FILL request) and the
	VFS -> file system REQ_PEEK message (new RW_PEEK_FILL flag, with which
	MFS and ext2 back file holes with real blocks for shared mappings).
	VM, VFS and the file system servers must be updated together: rebuild
	and install the whole boot image (make hdboot) and all of
	/service, not a single service, then reboot.

	libc gained Linux-compatible eventfd(2), timerfd_create(2) and
	signalfd(2) shims (<sys/eventfd.h>, <sys/timerfd.h>, <sys/signalfd.h>)
	and shm_open(3)/shm_unlink(3) backed by /dev/shm.  Create the
	directory if your /dev was not populated by a fresh install:

		# mkdir -p /dev/shm && chmod 1777 /dev/shm

	(/etc/rc.minix does this at boot, too, and empties /dev/shm like a
	tmpfs.)  select(2) is now restarted after a signal handler
	installed with SA_RESTART returns, as POSIX requires; use
	siginterrupt(3) to get the old EINTR behavior.

	Character devices can now be mmap(2)ed if their driver supports it:
	VFS asks the driver for the backing physical memory with the new
	FIOMMAP ioctl (<sys/ioc_file.h>) and VM maps it only if the driver
	itself holds the privilege for that memory.  This is another VM <->
	VFS protocol change (VMVF_PHYS FDLOOKUP replies), covered by the
	whole-boot-image update above.  The new "bochs" driver (QEMU/Bochs
	standard VGA, PCI 1234:1111) uses it to offer the DRM-lite display
	device /dev/drm0 (<sys/ioc_mdrm.h>); /usr/etc/rc starts it when the
	device is present.  On an existing system create the node with:

		# cd /dev && MAKEDEV drm0

	More Linux-compatible interfaces in libc: epoll(7) (<sys/epoll.h>,
	emulated over poll(2); level-triggered only, EPOLLET is refused),
	msync(2), and pthread_sigmask(3).  <pthread.h> now exists: it maps
	POSIX threads onto libmthread (cooperative user-level threads), and
	libpthread is installed as an alias of libmthread.  signalfd(2) now
	also works with the usual idiom of blocking the signals first: libc
	keeps signals owned by a signalfd unblocked internally while reporting
	them as blocked.  These are libc-only changes: dynamically linked
	programs pick them up, statically linked ones must be relinked.

20170303:
	The entire network stack has been replaced.  This requires two updates.
	First, BEFORE rebooting, either before or after rebuilding the system,
	copy the following new configuration files from /usr/src/etc into /etc:

		# cd /usr/src/etc
		# cp hosts inetd.conf named.conf nsswitch.conf /etc

	Second, AFTER rebooting, set up a new network configuration for your
	system.  Run netconf(8) and and follow its instructions:

		# netconf

	If you previously configured your network statically and you forgot its
	settings, take a look at /etc/rc.network.  That file and /etc/inet.conf
	are no longer used now.  See the ifconfig.if(5) manual page for more
	information about how network settings are specified going forward.

20170223:
	Edit your /etc/rc.conf to set rc_configured to YES.  If you do not,
	your system will refuse to enter multi-user mode after the next reboot.
	You can then still make the same edit from single user mode, but that
	is not as easy.

20170222:
	Because of naming conflicts, the MINIX 3 service(8) utility has been
	renamed to "minix-service".  After successfully rebuilding the system
	*and* rebooting (no earlier!), remove the old service utility:

		# rm /bin/service

	Better yet, use the postinstall(8) command to remove all files marked
	as obsolete.  It is not a bad idea to do this every now and then:

		# postinstall fix obsolete

	..but only ever after the reboot!  Either way, deleting the old service
	tool will avoid conflicts with the same-named NetBSD utility later on.

20170218:
	Before starting a build, you will have to update your master.passwd and
	group files in /etc.  This is needed because we have switched over from
	the old set of MINIX users and groups to the new set of NetBSD users
	and groups, as needed by various imported tools.  Follow these steps.

	1. Make a back-up copy of your old master.passwd and group files:

		# cp /etc/master.passwd /etc/master.passwd~
		# cp /etc/group /etc/group~

	2. Install /usr/src/etc/master.passwd to /etc/master.passwd, and
	   install /usr/src/etc/group to /etc/group.  If you have NOT created
	   any local user accounts yourself, for example because you are
	   upgrading a fresh MINIX 3 installation right away, all you need to
	   do is copy over those two files:

		# cp /usr/src/etc/master.passwd /etc/master.passwd
		# cp /usr/src/etc/group /etc/group

	   If you DO have custom user accounts or groups, you need to merge
	   their lines (and only those!) into the new master.passwd and group
	   files manually.  If you are unsure whether you have such custom
	   entries, you can use the following commands:

		# cd /usr/src
		# git show 68804c2:etc/master.passwd | diff -u - /etc/master.passwd
		# git show 68804c2:etc/group | diff -u - /etc/group

	   This should show any changes compared to the old two templates.  If
	   you have such changes, you need to reapply them after copying over
	   the new two templates.  The following procedure shows one approach:

		# cd /usr/src
		# cp /etc/master.passwd /tmp/my-passwd
		# vi /tmp/my-passwd
		<remove any lines from /tmp/my-passwd that have no password>
		# cp /usr/src/etc/master.passwd /etc/master.passwd
		# cat /tmp/my-passwd >> /etc/master.passwd
		# rm /tmp/my-passwd

	   ..and follow a similar procedure for /etc/group.

	3. Once the new files are in place, update all derived password files:

		# pwd_mkdb -p /etc/master.passwd

	   If you did everything right so far, the following command:

		# postinstall check uid gid

	   ..should show "postinstall checks passed: uid gid" and no failures.

	4. Now perform a full build as usual:

		# cd /usr/src
		# make build

	   As part of the build, all /dev files will get new proper ownership.

	5. There may still be files that are owned by users and groups that now
	   have been renumbered.  It is advisable to run at least the following
	   three commands to apply the renumbering to the most common files:

		# find / -user 2 -exec chown bin \{\} \;
		# find / -user 5 -exec chown uucp \{\} \;
		# find / -user 9998 -exec chown games \{\} \;

	   If you have installed custom programs, you may want to check whether
	   they are not affected by the change.  This should be rare, though.

	Until a subsequent reboot, various running services will show up in
	ps(1) output and /proc as owned by "postfix".  This is no problem.

20170122:
	MINIX 3 did not import NetBSD's passwd.conf file, which makes the
	userland fall back by default to the old, broken UNIX password hashing
	algorithm. Among other problems, it truncates passwords to the first
	eight characters.

	Please install /etc/passwd.conf and reset your passwords with passwd.

20160702:
	Some tools are required to generate the locale resources which are
	embedded into libintl, which is why you need to run the following:

	for t in gencat locale mkcsmapper mkesdb mklocale
	do
		make -C /usr/src/usr.bin/${t} all install
	done

	Then the usual "make build".

20150623:
	In order to use the new Unix98 PTYs, and to ensure that the test set
	continues to pass, please add the following line to your /etc/fstab:

	"none	/dev/pts	ptyfs	rw,rslabel=ptyfs	0	0"

